"FILE HOLDS THE TABLES, SO THE ACCESSOR HAS TO UNDO THE LAYOUT. Backus carries a FILE cell and declines to structure it; Codd supplies the structure and Halpin the design, which is what AREST fills that hole with -- so FILE is the RMAP projection, each fact type a relational cell whose rows are <CELL, key, dependent columns> (each entity its own cell, the 3NF row of facts depending on its key). A POPULATION is a set of facts, though: <draft, Sale>, not <CELL, draft, <Sale>>. Returning the storage form is how every state-machine reader saw an empty population while the fact was sitting in the store -- system:initial_status answered nothing for a Sale whose <draft, Sale> row was right there. The accessor converts; the readers ask for facts.",
"A POPULATION ROW IS A ROOT-TO-LEAF PATH. FILE holds each fact type as relational cells <CELL, key, dependents>, and the dependents of a ternary are themselves cells: <CELL o1 <<CELL widget <2>> <CELL gadget <1>>>>. The old ast:pop_row prepended the key to that LIST, which is right only one level deep -- correct for a binary by accident of depth, and for a ternary it gave one row per FIRST ROLE with cell structure sitting where values belong, so SaleContainsItemInQuantity answered 2 rows over a population of 3. These walk the tree instead: a cell contributes its key to every row its children produce, and children that are not cells are one row of values.",
"THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS CHANGED 227 OF 228 POPULATION READS, and the reason is worth keeping. It flattened with cat, and cat is BINARY -- it appends N(1) to N(2) and ignores the rest, dying on a one-element sequence. Over a two-child cell that is indistinguishable from a flatten, which is exactly the ternary in front of me, so the walk looked right while every longer list lost its tail and every empty one raised. main:flat is the seeded fold that actually flattens. The shape FILE returns was never the problem; the combinator was.",
"A UNARY FACT IS A ONE-TUPLE, and FILE does not store it as one. A unary population is projected as a flat list of VALUES -- <system:ev_cols, system:entity_view> -- which is the natural shape for a one-column table and is not a fact. Every other arity comes back as a tuple, and derive answers unary heads as one-tuples too, so the accessor was the only thing in the system that disagreed with itself: law:closed compared 29 rows against 29 rows and called them different. The non-cell branch below wraps a VALUE, so it becomes the row it stands for and main:flat has something to flatten instead of undoing the wrap it just made. A non-cell that is already a SEQUENCE is already a row and is passed through -- case:fetchpop-nested is the one that says so, where FILE carries F1 as <<a>, <b>> and wrapping twice would answer <<<a>>, <<b>>>.",
