"THE ROLE-1 PLAYER OF A FACT TYPE -- its subject. The write path needs it to decide whether a committed create BIRTHS a governed entity: engine.py sm_init_entity reads the player at role position ONE, because the key of the row being written is that role filler and a birth is a key with no status row yet. Distinct from system:governed_player, which answers whichever player a machine governs wherever it sits: here the POSITION is what fixes the answer, since the row being written puts its key first. Distinct also from system:mf_evpos, which runs the other way, position from player. A fact type with no role at position 1 answers bottom rather than a name: there is no subject to birth, and inventing one would seed a status against a key nobody wrote.",

"THE GOVERNING NOUN OF A NOUN. A machine may govern a noun directly or through the governedBy closure, so the write path resolves the noun before asking which status fact type holds its column. An UNGOVERNED noun answers ITSELF, and that default is the whole subtlety: it is not a missing value to be guarded against but the ordinary case -- a noun no closure mentions governs itself, so the same lookup serves both and the caller needs no branch. Answering phi instead would make every ungoverned noun look like a lookup failure, and the write path would stop birthing statuses for exactly the entities that have no subtyping.",

"THE STATUS FACT TYPE OF A NOUN -- which column holds its machine status. A noun with NO machine answers PHI, and that is deliberately different from system:role1_player, which bottoms: a noun without a machine is the ordinary case, most nouns in most apps, while a fact type with no role at position 1 is a compile fault. The two misses are not the same kind of thing and answering them alike would either bottom every ordinary write or swallow a real fault. Distinct from system:repr_statusft, which reads the same population for the representation and does not take this pair.",

"THE MACHINE OF A NOUN, GOVERNING NOUN FIRST. smDef rows read <machine, noun>, and the write path asks for the machine of the GOVERNING noun, falling back to the noun itself. The order is the meaning: a subtype governed by a supertype takes the SUPERTYPE machine, and looking the plain noun up first would find a machine the subtype only appears to have and seed the wrong initial status. The fallback still matters because governing_noun answers a noun ITSELF when no closure mentions it, and a noun may carry a machine under a name the closure never rewrites. No machine either way answers PHI: a noun without a machine is ordinary, like system:status_ft and unlike system:role1_player.",

"THE INITIAL STATUS OF A MACHINE. Read from the DECLARED population StatusIsInitialInStateMachineDefinition rather than from the effective one: a machine may also have a ROOTED status the base derives, and law:machine rests on the difference. What a birth lands is the declared initial, because a status derived from having no inbound transition is a fact about the graph, not a statement about where an entity starts. A machine with no declared initial answers PHI and the write path births nothing -- a machine whose start nobody stated has no defensible status to seed, and picking the first status by position would depend on authoring order.",

"DOES THIS ENTITY ALREADY CARRY A STATUS. The write path births an initial status only for an entity that has none, so this is the guard that makes a birth happen ONCE. It matches on the KEY, position 1 of the status population, not on the whole row: a status row is <key, status> and an entity that has already moved on carries a DIFFERENT status, so comparing rows would find no match and birth a second, contradictory row for the same entity. The one-write idempotence of the write path rests on this reading the key alone.",

"IS AN ABSORBED SLOT FILLED. An absorbed wide row carries one column per absorbed fact type, and the write path must know whether THIS entity already has a value there before it seeds one. The absent markers are the empty string, the HASH, and phi -- three of them, and that is not the same set the sparse populations use, where empty and phi mean absent and no hash appears. The hash is the wide row own marker for a column that exists in the layout but holds nothing for this entity, so a check written against the sparse markers reads a hash as a VALUE and refuses to seed a status the entity does not have. A row too SHORT to reach the position answers false rather than bottom: a wide row is ragged when the layout grew after it was written, and a missing tail is an unfilled slot, not a fault.",

"THE ROLES A NOUN PLAYS, with each fact type reading template and this noun POSITION in it. What the system can say about a noun starts here: not the fact types that mention it, but the roles it fills, which is why the position rides along -- Person in position 1 of Person_manages_Person is the manager and in position 2 the managed, and a listing that dropped the position would say the same thing twice about a ring fact type. A fact type with no reading contributes the EMPTY template rather than being skipped: the noun still plays that role, and dropping it would make the participation depend on whether anyone wrote a reading. The template is looked up per role rather than joined once, so a fact type the noun plays twice reports both positions against the same reading.",

"WHICH TABLE HOLDS A FACT TYPE ROWS. Reads the RMAP partition pairs <table, fact type> and answers the table, or the FACT TYPE ITSELF when no pair absorbs it. That default is the rule and not a fallback: rule 1 gives a fact type with a spanning uniqueness constraint or none at all its own table, so being absent from the partition IS having your own table, and the caller compares the answer against the fact type to learn whether it was absorbed. Answering phi for the unabsorbed case would make every rule-1 fact type look unrouted and the write path would have nowhere to put the commonest shape in any model.",

"IS THIS FACT TYPE A MACHINE TRIGGER. Asked all over the hosts and answered by scanning smTrigger for the fact type in the SECOND column -- the row is <machine, fact type>, so a scan of the first column answers about machines and would call every fact type a trigger in an app with one machine, or none in an app with none. The question decides real branches: a trigger fact type takes the gated create rather than the bulk install (system:replay_barrier), and create_spec builds it a machine, mealy and links recipe that a plain fact type does not get. One statement of it here rather than eleven scans in engine.py and their transliteration in rust.",

"EVERY TRIGGER FACT TYPE IN THE STORE, distinct and SORTED. system:is_trigger answers the question about one fact type; this answers the set, which is what a replay needs before it can decide which entries barrier (system:replay_barrier takes it) and what the compile-time fold walks. The sort is NOT decoration and not already done: rmap:distinct_at preserves dedup order, not alphabetical -- given b then a it answers b then a -- so the ordering has to be asked for. It matters because this list drives a greedy walk whose result depends on the order events are considered in, so an unsorted set makes the fold depend on the order fact types happened to be declared.",

