"WHETHER A RULE CAN PRODUCE A NEW FACT AT ALL, decided without evaluating it. MEASURED AND NOT USED PER RULE: gating derive:new_for on this cost 0.9% on the metamodel derivation and 1.2% over the 570 cases, on three separate encodings, because firing an empty-source rule is already an ALPHA over an empty list and deciding not to costs about the same as doing it. MEASURED FIVE WAYS AND NOT WIRED IN: gating derive:new_for on it moved law:rules and the 570-case sweep between -2% and +2.5%, which is noise, INCLUDING the version that tests both delta sources and skips 17 of the 39 metamodel rules. The reason is in derive:round_stats: the rules a source-emptiness test can reach are the CHEAP ones -- 11 proj, 4 minus, 2 cmp -- while 17 join and 4 joinon firings carry the cost, and a join is only skippable when BOTH its deltas are empty, which on a real store is rare. So this def stays as the answer to a question worth being able to ask, pinned by four cases, and derive:new_for fires unconditionally. derive:run_layer is WHILE (delta non-empty) derive:round over the state <rules, <full, delta>>, so the fixpoint was already semi-naive per LAYER; nothing was semi-naive per RULE, and derive:round fires every rule every round. Measured on the base: of 143 rules with sources, 132 read only EMPTY fact types. Sam: memoization is good, but not running something unnecessarily is better -- memoising a firing that produces nothing still runs it once, and this decides it without evaluating anything.",
"THE TEST IS derive:srcd, WHICH READS THE DELTA STORE; derive:srcf reads the full one, and they take different positions of that pair. An empty delta on a source is semi-naive evaluation is own skip condition.",
"WHY A WHITELIST AND NOT AN EXCLUSION LIST -- this cost a law. delta_eval dispatches seven tags and then FALLS THROUGH to a join, so the tag set is open: the metamodel rules are written with a join tag that appears in none of the seven, and any future form lands in the same fallback. A rule whose form this def does not recognize must fire. Naming the arms that are safe fails closed; naming the arms that are unsafe fails silently, on exactly the forms nobody thought of.",
"AND THE TWO-SOURCE ARMS ARE NOT SAFE, WHICH IS NOT VISIBLE FROM THEIR FIRST SOURCE. joinon and the join fallback are both dedup . cat . <term1, term2>, and the two terms are the two delta directions: term1 is srcd(left) against srcf(right), term2 is srcf(left) against srcd(right). BOTH DIRECTIONS LIVE IN ONE ARM rather than in two compiled delta-rules. Testing the left delta alone therefore skips a rule whose RIGHT side changed, which is precisely how a transitive closure stops closing -- law rules-reproduce caught it on StatusReachesStatus, and 578 cases did not.",
