"TWO LAWS FOR A WEEK OF DEFECTS (2026-09-07). writable: the store carries no alethic violation, because Thm 1 refuses every write on one that does, and a check that reports zero errors on such a store is how 272 of them were found one refusal at a time. file-is-projection: for every declared fact type, every row the descriptor's fifth slot holds is read back through system:pop_rows -- FILE is the projection of the source (main:file_of) and a writer that touches the source without rebuilding it (the screen's submit, fire and retract each did, in their own copies of the slot walk) is the defect this reports. Subset, not equality: the reflected and derived populations (roles, readings, world assumptions) live in FILE and top-level cells with no source row at all, which is their home by design. Both laws are read over the booted store and, in law:validate_gate, over the store a submit answers.",
"A TENANT IS A CELL WHOSE CONTENTS IS AN ENTIRE STORE (Samuel, 2026-09-11: auto.dev subdomain apps should work together -- PM should be able to assign engineering tasks escalated from support). That is not a design to invent: AREST.tex states it and proves what follows from it. Backus 14.7 gives `a cell in one store may contain another entire store`; the paper's Tenancy paragraph makes a tenant exactly that, so P partitions into per-tenant blocks; and Prop. tenant (Store-relative tenant isolation) proves that for stores in SIBLING tenant cells, no expression formed from the up and down addressing relative to one denotes a cell of the other, because those resolve only cells of their store argument. Visibility follows containment: a parent reaches into its children because their stores are cells in its own, and a child cannot address upward or sideways. Lambda had NONE of it -- the word `tenant` appeared once in this file, in a comment about UI panes -- while ast:pop_iscell and ast:pop_sub already recognised and expanded a cell whose contents is a population. So the pieces were here and the surface was not.",
"WHICH DECIDES THE THING IT WAS ASKED TO DECIDE. Four subdomain apps that must work together cannot do it by addressing each other: siblings are not forbidden to one another, they are UNADDRESSABLE, and the paper is explicit that this needs no tenant filter because it is a property of the shape of the state. So an escalation that support hands to pm, and a task pm assigns to engineering, are facts of the PARENT store that contains all four, not messages between four stores. That is also the answer to whether an app is one module per store or one module over many: the object contains isolated copies of itself, so it is one object with the apps as cells.",
"AND THE LAW IS THE PROOF'S OWN CONTENT, not a proxy for it. Prop. tenant rests on `up and down resolve only cells of their store argument`, so the checkable statement is that a fetch over a tenant's store answers THAT tenant's own cells, name by name, and nothing else: tenant:own reads the cells directly, tenant:fetched goes through ast:fetch, and law:tenant_local holds when they agree. A leak in either direction -- a sibling's cell reachable, or one of its own unreachable -- breaks the equality. It is VACUOUS on a store with no tenant cells, which is every store today, so it costs nothing until there is something to isolate; a store that has tenants pays one fetch per cell of each tenant.",
"AND FILE IS NOT A TENANT, WHICH THE SHAPE ALONE CANNOT SAY. A cell whose contents is a list of cells describes FILE exactly as well as it describes a tenant: FILE holds one cell per populated fact type. The first cut of this said T for both and named FILE among the tenants of every store. The discriminator is the paper's own sentence -- `Every node is a full instance, since the closure corollary holds per store` -- so a tenant is not merely a cell containing cells, it is a cell containing a STORE, and a store is what carries a FILE. FILE's own contents are population cells and none of them is a FILE, so it fails the test it would otherwise pass. A cell that has never been given a FILE is not yet a full instance and is correctly not a tenant.",
