"THE PLATFORM NAVIGATES THROUGH ONE SERVED VERB (2026-10-01). A platform is a shim that registers its paired controls, hands each event to lambda and renders what lambda answers (Sam, 2026-09-20), and MonoCross names the event: MXContainer.Navigate matches the address to a controller, loads its model and outputs the view the platform registered for it. navigate is that event for every platform, one evaluation per navigation. Its operand is the request and the cells. The request is <stacks, address, widths, registered>: the history stack population the platform holds (its form factor, the stacks iFactr`s PaneManager keeps per pane), the address it was asked to go to, the width of each pane as <pane, width> pairs, and the render names it registered. The answer is <stacks, style, panes, unpaired, refusal>: the stacks ui:navp leaves, ui:style as its slots, each registered pane as <pane, placed rows> -- ui:pane_view then ui:arrange, the rows the Swing container draws -- law:unpaired over the names the platform registered, so the gate is asked of lambda and not restated by the platform, and an empty text. A missing request element takes its default: the master and detail stacks, the root, ui:style`s frameW, no registrations. A WRITE IS REFUSED HERE AND NAMED, with the stacks as they were: an address headed fire, submit or retract asserts through ui:navp, and ui:apply asserts the trigger fact through main:assert0 with no validate leg, so a fire from a screen does not take the gate a POST of the same fact takes.",
"A SCREEN WRITE IS A REQUEST TO THE UNIFORM INTERFACE (Sam, 2026-10-01: screen writes conform to the AREST paper, Backus, Codd, Fielding and Halpin). navigate refused every write by name, and before it the screen`s write asserted the trigger fact through main:assert0 (ui:apply), with no validation and no performer, so a fire from a screen did not take the gate a POST of the same fact takes and a transition`s bound Predicate never ran. A write address is now a request of main:api, the one interface every write takes (Fielding), so it is resolved, derived to the fixpoint, validated and emitted (Def 6) and committed exactly when it has no alethic violation, its deontic ones warning (Thm 1): a submit, <submit, collection, <key, value>...>, the form`s values as the platform sends them with the link (iFactr`s IListView.Submit), is a POST of the collection with those pairs, and a fire, <fire, event fact type, type, entity, <key, value>...>, is a POST of the event fact type whose roles the entity fills where its type plays and the pairs fill by player elsewhere. navigate answers the frame over the store the write left, with the request`s own stacks moved only by the write: a submit puts the new entity where its entry form stood. A committed write lays out every pane, since any pane may show what it changed, and a refused one lays out none. The answer is main:api`s outcome as an IAlert, refused, or committed with its deontic warnings, the representation the API itself answers, a text it answers as a JSON string being that text, and nothing when it committed clean. Over HTTP the answer carries the write`s status and, when it committed, the store, so the serving host adopts it, emits the changed populations and runs the performers exactly as for a POST. The client holds the stacks; nothing of the navigation is kept. The old arms go with this: ui:navp_write, ui:apply and the screen`s ui:retract asserted without the gate, ui:violscreen and ui:violsay drew the refusal the alert now carries, and ui:navpe, the Java container`s entry to them, which its registration surface replaces. A retract is not a screen`s and is refused as one the interface does not serve here. Operand of ui:navigate_step: <request, store, caller>, answering <frame, store, status, committed>; of ui:write: <store, address, caller>, answering main:api`s <body, status, store>.",
