"ONE REQUEST, ONE ANSWER: <body, status>. Operand <store, method, resource, caller, links>. This is the entry a serving host calls, and the whole of what it does is lambda -- admit the method against http:method_kinds, restrict the links to what this caller may do, render the representation with render:json, and take the status from the outcome. A host that binds a socket adds no branch: it reads a request, applies this, writes the two answers. Sam: nothing custom should wrangle it into a HATEOAS server besides the native registrations required for serving. AREST.tex:260 is why that is possible -- every component of repr(e), the selectors on its facts, the derived facts, the violations and links(e), is (rho f):P for some object f, so there is nothing left for a host to compose. AN UNADMITTED METHOD ANSWERS 405 AND AN EMPTY BODY rather than an empty link set, because a client that cannot tell `you may do nothing here` from `I do not serve that verb` will retry forever.",
"READING A POPULATION BACK, which is the half of REST this had never served. GET answered Theorem 2-s nav leg -- the affordances of a resource -- and no method returned ROWS, so a fact could be written and never read: the MCP surface could POST into any of 246 fact types and query none of them. A store you can only write is half a store, and it is the half that does not help. http:status already named `read` and answered 200 for it, so the vocabulary had been waiting.",
"A FILLER CAN SKIP A ROLE, which prefix matching could not. theta:zip compares from the left, so a bare list of fillers restricts roles 1..n and can never ask about role 2 alone -- which functions are superseded BY derive:stratified was unaskable, and that is half the questions worth asking of a binary. An EMPTY filler element is the wildcard: it matches whatever sits in that role, so <, derive:stratified> restricts on role 2 and says nothing about role 1. The prefix behaviour survives untouched because zip still truncates, so a SHORT list is the same as trailing wildcards and a bare read still matches everything.",
